<section class="panel build-log-panel<%= " panel--quiet" if @entries.empty? %>" id="project-build-log">
  <%# ONE PANEL-HEAD SHAPE ACROSS ALL FOUR PANELS (UI-cohesion slice 5): title, optional muted
      meta, actions right. This and Tasks carried a BARE <h2> while Files and Publish wrapped
      theirs, so `.panel-head`'s alignment and gap applied to half the page — and this panel
      additionally put its one action on the LINE BELOW the title (§2.10), a third arrangement again.
      THE WHOLE `<details>` GOES IN THE HEAD, not merely its summary: the two are one element. It is
      a DIRECT CHILD of `.panel-head` and not wrapped in `.panel-actions`, and that is not tidiness
      — a first draft put it inside one and the OPEN composer became a ~70px column squeezed beside
      the title, because `.panel-actions` is a flex item that shrinks and the form inherits whatever
      width is left. A screenshot said so; the reasoning that predicted otherwise was wrong. As a
      direct child, `pages/projects.css` can give the open state `flex-basis: 100%` and it takes the
      row beneath the title. Closed — its resting state — it is a button on the right like every
      other panel's action. %>
  <div class="panel-head">
    <h2><%= panel_title(@panel_layout, "build_log") %></h2>
    <details class="composer log-add">
      <%# THE PANELS' ONE COMPOSER PATTERN (2026-11-02, the UX audit's C4): the opener is "Add…" and
          the submit names the thing — "Add line", "Add connection", "Add release", "Add task", "Add
          sheet", "Add entry" — so the two never carry one label (the §1A.8 rule this composer
          first stated, when its opener and its submit both read "Add entry" 150px apart). %>
      <summary class="btn small" aria-label="Add a build log entry">Add…</summary>
      <%# scope: :build_log_entry — form_with on a NAMESPACED model (Maker::BuildLogEntry) derives
          `maker_build_log_entry[...]`, which the controller's params.require(:build_log_entry)
          rejects. Same trap as projects/_form.html.erb. %>
      <%= form_with model: @new_entry, scope: :build_log_entry,
                    url: project_build_log_entries_path(@project), class: "log-form" do |f| %>
        <%# A VISIBLE LABEL, NOT A PLACEHOLDER (the UX audit's §I): "What did you do?" was the field's
            only name and vanished at the first keystroke. The editor is a form-associated element,
            so a `<label for>` names it. %>
        <label class="composer-label" for="build-log-entry-body">What did you do?</label>
        <%= f.rich_text_area :body, id: "build-log-entry-body", attachments: "false" %>
        <%= f.submit "Add entry", class: "btn small" %>
      <% end %>
    </details>
  </div>
  <%= panel_intro(@panel_layout, "build_log") %>

  <%# QUIET AT ZERO (stage 4, S4-1): a build log with nothing in it is the composer alone. The
      sentence it replaces said only what the empty panel already showed, above an Add entry
      control that says it better. The class on the section is what pages/projects.css lays out. %>
  <% if @entries.any? %>
    <ol class="log-timeline">
      <% @entries.each do |entry| %>
        <%= render "log_entry", entry: %>
      <% end %>
    </ol>
  <% end %>
</section>
